#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158528 :tutorial/content "We're used to plotting curves where $y$ is a function of $x.$ However, we can also plot curves where $x$ is a function of $y.$\n\nFor example, consider the curve $x = y^2.$ To graph this curve, we can start by calculating the values of $x$ for some values of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-3 & \\,⟶\\,x=(-3)^{2}=9 \\\\ y=-2 & \\,⟶\\,x=(-2)^{2}=4 \\\\ y=-1 & \\,⟶\\,x=(-1)^{2}=1 \\\\ y=0 & \\,⟶\\,x={0}^{2}=0 \\\\ y=1 & \\,⟶\\,x={1}^{2}=1 \\\\ y=2 & \\,⟶\\,x={2}^{2}=4 \\\\ y=3 & \\,⟶\\,x={3}^{2}=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize these results in the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nSo, we have the following points:\n\n$$\n(9,-3), \\quad (4,-2), \\quad (1,-1), \\quad (0,0), \\quad (1,1), \\quad (4,2), \\quad (9,3).\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points and connect them with a smooth curve, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/ae/aec45789560229c23f0c86069bad46593a7d00a637f6f8da33d36634ae504e52.png)\n\nWe say that the equation of the curve is defined **implicitly**, because instead of the usual $y = f(x),$ we have $x = f(y).$ \n\n**Watch out!** Here, we are viewing $x=y^2$ not as a function but rather as an equation. A point belongs to the curve if and only if its coordinates satisfy the equation. The curve does not represent a function since it fails the vertical line test." :tutorial/id #uuid "3217d90b-5727-5445-a812-db001667675c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4707 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158529 :tutorial/content "Another way to plot $x = f(y)$ is to first plot the corresponding function $y = f(x)$ and then reflect it over the line $y=x.$\n\nTo illustrate, let's use this technique to plot the curve $x=y^2.$ To get the corresponding function, we swap $x$ and $y$ and get $y=x^2.$ This curve is familiar and much easier to graph:\n\n![](images/af/afca2357f9d26876cedc7fb1d059c95c8906d9144f2fdb17dd2f61eec878a196.png)\n\nNow, to get the curve $x=y^2,$ we just have to reflect the curve $y=x^2$ over the line $y=x,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/5b/5b91959bd83265ed0b606072b26029bd995f51a8128c14367af9013688db148c.png)\n\nYou may notice that this process is very similar to plotting the inverse of a function. In fact, if $y=f(x)$ is a one-to-one function, then the corresponding function $x = f(y)$ will always give the inverse function." :tutorial/id #uuid "ef9c9a8e-f3c3-57cf-936a-e4586282e650" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4708 :tutorial/title "Plotting X as a Function of Y Using a Reflection"}]}
